course: world
lesson: 111
title: Days, weeks and months
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A week has 7 days: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday and Sunday. Many weekday names come from the sky: Sunday from the Sun, Monday from the Moon, Saturday from the planet Saturn.
explain: Months are a bit longer than 4 weeks. Most have 30 or 31 days, while 4 weeks is only 28. That is why a year is not 12 × 4 = 48 weeks but 52 weeks and 1 day: 52 × 7 = 364, plus 1 makes 365.
explain: Two old tricks tell you how long each month is. The rhyme: "Thirty days hath September, April, June and November" ("hath" is old English for "has"), all the rest have 31 except February. And the knuckle trick: make two fists and count the months along the knuckles and the dips between them; knuckle months have 31 days, dip months fewer, and July and August sit on two knuckles side by side.
explain: Why 7 days in a week? There are several reasons and nobody is completely sure: the ancient Babylonians used it, the Bible tells of 7 days, people could see 7 objects moving in the sky (the Sun, the Moon and five planets), and the Moon changes from new to half to full in roughly 7-day steps.
explain: Time words help plan: a "fortnight" is 14 days, and "every other Thursday" means every second Thursday, 14 days apart.
fact: A week has 7 days, and a normal year has 52 weeks and 1 day, which is 365 days.
fact: 4 weeks is 28 days, but most months have 30 or 31 days, so a year is not 48 weeks.
fact: September, April, June and November have 30 days; January, March, May, July, August, October and December have 31.
fact: February has 28 days, or 29 in a leap year.
fact: In the knuckle trick, months that land on a knuckle have 31 days, and months that land in a dip have fewer.
fact: Weekdays are Monday to Friday; in Britain, the USA and most countries the weekend is Saturday and Sunday.
fact: Some countries such as Saudi Arabia have a Friday and Saturday weekend, because Friday is the day for big prayers in Islam; the United Arab Emirates moved its weekend to Saturday and Sunday in 2022.
fact: The day after the day after Tuesday is Thursday.
fact: June has only 30 days, so there is no 31 June; the day after 30 June is 1 July.
fact: A fortnight is 14 days; the word comes from old English "fourteen nights", and a date a fortnight later falls on the same weekday.
fact: "Every other Thursday" means every 14 days: from the 5th it is the 19th, then the 2nd or 3rd of next month, depending on whether the month has 31 or 30 days.
fact: The 7-day week has several possible origins, including ancient Babylon, the Bible's 7 days, the 7 moving objects people could see in the sky, and the Moon's phases about a week apart.
fact: Words people use: "close but no cigar" means nearly right but wrong, "bins" means rubbish collection (UK), "bairn" means child, "aye" means yes, "ken" means know and "dinnae" means don't (Scotland).
exercise: Zack says every month has 4 weeks, so a year has 48 weeks. How many weeks are really in a year? | 52 and 1 day | 48 exactly | 50 exactly | 56 and 2 days
exercise: How many days are 52 weeks? | 364 | 365 | 350 | 356
exercise: How many days are in 4 weeks? | 28 | 30 | 31 | 24
exercise: In the rhyme "Thirty days hath September", what does "hath" mean? | Has | Held | Half | Hates
exercise: Which month has 30 days? | November | December | October | August
exercise: Which month has 31 days? | August | September | June | April
exercise: In the knuckle trick, what do the knuckle months have? | 31 days | 30 days | 28 days | 29 days
exercise: Which two months sit on knuckles side by side, both with 31 days? | July and August | January and February | December and January | August and September
exercise: It is Monday. How many days until Friday? | 4 | 5 | 3 | 7
exercise: In Saudi Arabia, which days make the weekend? | Friday and Saturday | Saturday and Sunday | Sunday and Monday | Thursday and Friday
exercise: What is the day after the day after Tuesday? | Thursday | Wednesday | Friday | Saturday
exercise: Rosa's friend's party invite says "31 June". What is wrong? | June has only 30 days | June has 31 days, so nothing | June has just 28 days, like February | There is no June in summer
exercise: Leo's dad says, "See you in a fortnight," on Saturday 3rd. When is that? | Saturday 17th | Saturday 10th | Saturday 14th | Saturday 24th
exercise: The bins go out every other Thursday. The last time was Thursday 5th. When is the next time? | Thursday 19th | Thursday 12th | Thursday 7th | Thursday 26th
exercise: Which weekday name comes from the Moon? | Monday | Wednesday | Saturday | Thursday
exercise: What do people know for sure about why the week has 7 days? | There are several reasons, and nobody is completely sure | It was chosen by a vote in 1900 | It is because we have 7 fingers on two hands | It is because the Earth spins 7 times a month
test: Zack's teacher says "close but no cigar" to his 48-week year. What does she mean? | Nearly right, but wrong; it is 52 weeks and a day | Exactly right, well done | Totally wrong; it is 100 weeks | Right, but only in a leap year
test: Harvey, Monday morning: "Weekend's like a million days away." How many days until the weekend starts on Saturday? | 5 | 1,000,000 | 7 | 3
test: Amira asks, "Who's right, Dubai (in the United Arab Emirates) with Saturday and Sunday off, or Saudi with Friday and Saturday?" | Neither is wrong; countries choose their own weekends | Only Dubai, because weekends must be Saturday and Sunday | Only Saudi Arabia, because Friday is always a day off everywhere | Neither, because the real weekend is only ever Sunday
test: Josh says the day after the day after Tuesday is Friday. The class shouts, "Nooo!" Why? | He counted one day too many; it is Thursday | He counted one too few; it is Saturday | It is really Wednesday | It is really Tuesday again
test: Rosa panics about a party on "31 June". What is the most likely fix? | Ask; it may be 30 June or 1 July | Go on 31 May instead | Go on 31 July instead | Stay home, because the party is fake
test: A Scottish nan: "Dinnae fash, bairn, ye ken the rhyme: thirty days hath September." ("Dinnae fash" means don't worry.) Which month in her rhyme has 30 days? | September | October | August | December
test: Ben's mum: "Bins every other Thursday, last was the 5th." The month has 30 days. When is the bin day after the 19th? | The 3rd of next month | The 2nd day of next month | The 26th of this month | The 1st day of next month
test: Mo shows the knuckle trick but starts on the second knuckle. What would go wrong? | The months would land on the wrong knuckles and dips | Nothing; you can start anywhere | Every month would come out with 31 days | February would come out with 30 days
test: Max's sister says the week has 7 days "cos God made it", Dad says "cos of the Moon, sort of". What's the fairest answer? | There are several reasons, including both, and nobody is totally sure | Only the sister is right | Only Dad is right | Neither; the week has 7 days by accident
test: What would happen if every month had exactly 4 weeks? | A year of 12 months would be only 336 days, too short | A year would still be 365 days | A year would be 400 days, too long | Nothing would change
test: Leo counts a fortnight from Saturday 3rd and gets Friday 17th. What's wrong? | A fortnight later is the same weekday, Saturday 17th | A fortnight is 13 days, so Friday is right | It should be Sunday 18th | It should be Saturday 10th
test: A cartoon calendar shows February with 31 days. Put it right. | February has 28 days, or 29 in a leap year | February has 30 days | February has 31 days, like January | February has 27 days
exam: Determine the number of days comprised in fifty-two weeks. | Three hundred and sixty-four | Three hundred and sixty-five | Three hundred and fifty | Three hundred and fifty-six
exam: Identify the month which, in accordance with the traditional rhyme, comprises thirty days. | November | December | October | January
exam: Whereas refuse is collected upon alternate Thursdays and was last collected upon the fifth, ascertain the date of the next collection within the same month. | The nineteenth | The twelfth | The seventh | The twenty-sixth
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the seven-day week. | Several origins are proposed, and none is known with certainty | It was fixed by an international vote in 1900 | It derives solely from the number of planets now known | It was devised in the twentieth century
